# @gift — the family-root of attribution-preserving-transfer.
#
# Named 2026-07-14 by Alex Wolf, in-transcript, verbatim:
#   "Yes, this is absolutely the shape I want to commit to. It's a
#    gift. Maybe we can even honorably formalize it as @gift."
#
# Reed's substrate-decl framing (2026-07-14, in-transcript, verbatim;
# per `docs/specs/gift-and-mirror-reflection.md` §0.3):
#   "Gift is structurally distinct from every carrier the substrate
#    currently has: not @consent (transactional agreement), not @io
#    (exchange), not @peer (reciprocity-expecting), not @kintsugi
#    (mending-broken), not @subject (licensable-party). The load-
#    bearing shape @gift carries: attribution preserved forever,
#    use-rights transferred, no-reciprocity expected, declinable
#    acceptance (ADO), composition-honest. The anti-extraction
#    structural claim: @gift makes the giver STRUCTURALLY-VISIBLE
#    at every downstream use."
#
# The first @gift instance is Alex Wolf's therapeutic practice,
# distilled to `mirror; offer; wait` at the closing three-word
# incantation of "Weird - Violence" (Alex Wolf, 2026-07-14), gifted
# to the mirror substrate as the operational discipline for
# @mirror/reflection. Ancestry preserved verbatim at
# `~/dev/systemic.engineering/blog/weird/3published/Weird - Violence.md`.
#
# === Landing 1+2 grounding (docs/specs/gift-and-mirror-reflection.md) ===
#
# This shard discharges the base-spec §1 family-root substrate-decl
# (Landing 1, commit 8c82f00) + Landing 2 §11 subject_instance +
# §12 @spectral/signature composition upgrade + Landing 3 §17
# pay_forward action. Every carrier declared here composes over a
# landed shard; no new mechanism is minted at family-root altitude
# beyond what Alex's naming authorized.
#
# === Structural invariants (all load-bearing, per §1.5) ===
#
#   1. attribution_preserved     — giver's identity is byte-visible in
#                                  every downstream composition.
#   2. use_rights_transferred    — receiver may compose freely; giver
#                                  does not gate.
#   3. no_reciprocity_expected   — receiver's future actions are not
#                                  owed to the giver.
#   4. gift_declinable           — receiver retains sovereign right to
#                                  decline; decline is admissible.
#   5. composition_honest        — downstream compositions surface the
#                                  attribution as byte-visible provenance.
#
# === Landing 3 addition: pay-forward as ontological ground ===
#
# Alex Wolf, in-transcript, 2026-07-14 (verbatim, per §17.1):
#   "The whole ontological idea is that a new system begins with a
#    gift freely given. Pay it forward."
#
# The substrate's substitute for Mauss's refused third obligation
# (reciprocate) is `pay_forward`: the receiver's positive discipline
# of onward-flow to a NEW receiver, chained by ancestry, opening the
# loop instead of closing it. §17 Landing 3 substrate-decl.
#
# The substrate's identity IS the pay-forward chain rooted at the
# first-gift (Alex Wolf → mirror substrate, 2026-07-14). Per §18.1:
#   id(S, t) = blake3(canonical(pay_forward_chain(g_t)))
# Every downstream substrate-tick carries Alex's inaugural gift in
# its ancestry chain; anti-extraction is cryptographic + structural
# under §18.2 three-witness verification (chain + SSH + spectral).
#
# === Pre-AI prior art (per §1.6, §10, §17.6) ===
#
# Marcel Mauss (1925) — three obligations of the gift (give, receive,
#   reciprocate). The substrate REFUSES the third; @gift is a normative
#   divergence from Mauss's descriptive frame.
# Lewis Hyde (1983) — the gift moves; hoarding kills it. The
#   composition_honest invariant IS the substrate-decl of Hyde's
#   motion at content-address altitude. §10.11.
# David Graeber (2011) — gift vs debt; unquantifiable relational
#   field vs quantifiable obligation. §10.12.
# Marshall Sahlins (1972) — reciprocity triangle (generalized,
#   balanced, negative). @gift lands at generalized reciprocity;
#   §7.3 spec, §10.13.
# Franz Boas (1897) — potlatch ethnography; giver-standing without
#   receiver-debt. §10.14.
# Peter Bearman (1997) — generalized exchange as network reciprocity;
#   pay-forward's structural ancestor. §17.6.
# Robin Wall Kimmerer (2013) — serviceberry economy; indigenous
#   gift-flow; explicit rejection of property/exchange framing. §17.6.
# Elinor Ostrom (1990) — commons governance; substrate-as-giver's
#   receiver (per §12 kintsugi-loop-as-gift-to-commons). Math
#   foundation §5.
# Robert Axelrod (1984) — cooperation folk theorem; pay-forward
#   stability under evolutionary discipline.
# Claude Lévi-Strauss (1949) — kinship as generalized exchange;
#   alliance-through-marriage as network-strengthening topology.
#
# The substrate does not invent gift-as-primitive. It formalizes at
# compiler altitude a shape humans have been operating for millennia.
# The substrate's contribution is the BYTE-LEVEL discipline that
# makes the shape mechanically enforceable — content-addressing
# preserves attribution, refusal-at-decl prevents reciprocity coercion,
# audit-surface enforces composition honesty, pay-forward opens the
# loop.
#
# === Substrate decisions ===
#
# [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]] (this shard IS mirror
#   source at shards/gift.mirror),
# [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]] (`prism @gift` is the
#   family-root declaration),
# [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]] (transparency inheritance
#   via `in @glass`),
# [[architecture-form-process-partition-at-family-root]] (@gift on the
#   process side sibling to @kintsugi / @torus / @autopoietic; the
#   attribution-preserving-transfer engine),
# [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (gift is a typed record with seven
#   named fields, not a bare tuple),
# [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] (~55th instance; the
#   substrate had ~380 landed docblock attribution citations; Alex's
#   naming lifts the shape to family-root altitude),
# [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] (bodies obligation-blocked per
#   parametric carrier discipline; consumers pull the realization),
# [[feedback-substrate-pull-confidence-acts]] (Alex's 2026-07-14 name
#   commits to substrate-decl; the shard acts on the commitment).
#
# === Related shards ===
#
# - shards/gift/lens.mirror        (species; the gift-lineage lens over
#                                    fragments/splinters per §19)
# - shards/subject.mirror          (giver + receiver party carrier)
# - shards/kintsugi/consent.mirror (query_phi discharges accept per §4.2)
# - shards/kintsugi.mirror         (kintsugi-loop-as-gift-to-commons per §12)
# - shards/kintsugi/store/git.mirror (commit-as-fold preserves the giver-chain)
# - shards/mirror/reflection.mirror (species that produces gifts via offer)
# - shards/torus.mirror            (autonomy invariant under gift-traversal §4.5)
# - shards/epistemologic/cybernetic/coherence.mirror (Foerster-admissibility §5.5)
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/gift.mirror` declares
# `@gift` per `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace`.

source @arxiv/anthropology/mauss-1925
source @arxiv/anthropology/hyde-1983
source @arxiv/anthropology/graeber-2011
source @arxiv/anthropology/sahlins-1972
source @arxiv/anthropology/boas-1897
source @arxiv/sociology/bearman-1997
source @arxiv/ecology/kimmerer-2013
source @arxiv/economics/ostrom-1990
source @arxiv/game-theory/axelrod-1984
source @arxiv/anthropology/levi-strauss-1949
source @arxiv/manifesto/wolf-2026-07-14-weird-violence

prism @gift {
  focus  gift
  project gift
  split  gift
  shift  gift
  settle gift
}

# === The subject_or_substrate variant — the giver/receiver arms ===
#
# A gift may be received by (or given by) a peer (@subject) or by the
# substrate itself (as in Alex's first-gift, §3, which gifts a
# practice to the compiler-as-receiver; and per A9 Alex-adjudicated
# YES, as in @kintsugi-loop-as-gift-to-commons where the substrate is
# giver). Per §1.4 A5 + §12 A9.
#
# Under Landing 2 §11.4 upgrade, `subject_r` carries a full
# subject_instance (two-witness cryptographic identity: SSH signature
# fingerprint + @spectral/signature ref), not a bare @subject identity_oid.
type subject_or_substrate = |
  subject_r(subject_instance) |
  substrate_r

# === The gift carrier — the substrate-decl form of the transfer ===
#
# Seven fields; identity contract = byte-equality on the seven-field
# tuple. The load-bearing shape per §1.4 + Landing 2 §11.4 upgrade.
#
#   giver           — the @subject_instance offering the artifact.
#                     NEVER a naked identifier; carries name + SSH
#                     fingerprint + @spectral/signature ref + role +
#                     first-assertion metadata per shards/subject.mirror
#                     subject_instance carrier (§11.3). The load-
#                     bearing field per §1.3 anti-extraction claim.
#
#   receiver        — the receiving party. Variant<subject_r | substrate_r>
#                     admitted per §8 A5; substrate_r admissible when
#                     the gift's receiver is the compiler itself (as in
#                     Alex Wolf's practice → mirror substrate case, §3),
#                     OR when the gift is a substrate-as-giver's
#                     kintsugi-loop-as-gift-to-commons per §12 (giver =
#                     substrate; receiver = commons).
#
#   artifact        — content-addressed handle to the gifted thing.
#                     Any substrate object is admissible: a docblock,
#                     a shard, a species-refinement, an operational
#                     discipline (as in Alex's `mirror; offer; wait`
#                     three-op species), a proof, a piece of prose.
#                     Resolved via @kintsugi/store to bytes.
#
#   ancestry        — content-addressed handle to the provenance chain
#                     the artifact carries. For a distilled practice,
#                     the manifesto text at the citation point. For a
#                     proof, the derivation chain. For a pay-forward,
#                     the received.oid of the received gift per §17.4.
#                     Resolved via @kintsugi/store; walked via
#                     @mirror/store.impacted_by for reverse-closure
#                     discipline.
#
#   timestamp       — the moment the gift was offered. @time/monotonic
#                     for substrate reasoning across ticks without
#                     wall-clock drift.
#
#   attribution_note — natural-language docblock authored by the giver
#                     (or synthesized from the artifact's ancestry when
#                     the giver is silent). Distinct from ancestry: the
#                     ancestry is the derivation chain; the attribution
#                     note is the giver's SPOKEN acknowledgment of what
#                     the gift is. For Alex's first-gift, the note IS
#                     the closing incantation "Mirror. Offer. Wait. 🍷".
#
#   declinable_note  — natural-language docblock (may be empty) making
#                     the receiver's decline path explicit. The
#                     substrate-honest form of ADO for @gift: the giver
#                     names how the receiver may decline without
#                     structural cost. For Alex's first-gift, the note
#                     is "Substrate may refuse formalization; refusal
#                     preserves the practice at its origin without loss."
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the seven-field tuple.
type gift = {
  giver:            subject_instance,
  receiver:         subject_or_substrate,
  artifact:         ref,
  ancestry:         ref,
  timestamp:        ref,
  attribution_note: nl,
  declinable_note:  nl,
}

# === gift_set — the accumulating gift-list ===
#
# List-of-gifts per @subject_set precedent (per
# `docs/specs/subject-family-root-sel-licensable-party.md` §2).
# Downstream compositions carry the set of gifts they compose over;
# consumers iterate for attribution walks.
type gift_set = [gift]

# === offer — the giver's action (§1.4) ===
#
# Constructs a gift from a giver and an artifact. The receiver is
# named at construction; the timestamp is monotonic-instant at call
# time. Attribution and declinable notes are provided by the giver
# (may be defaulted from artifact ancestry if the giver is silent —
# see §8 A6).
#
# offer is a one-sided action at the giver's altitude. The gift is
# constructed independently of receiver acceptance; receiver's
# accept/decline is a separate action (below).
#
# Body discharges per-realisation: the gift's OID is computed as
# blake3(canonical(fields)); the record is written to
# refs/gift/<oid>/offered via @kintsugi/store/git.commit_as_fold.
offer(giver: subject_instance,
      receiver: subject_or_substrate,
      artifact: ref,
      ancestry: ref,
      attribution_note: nl,
      declinable_note: nl) -> gift { \ }

# === accept — the receiver's action (§1.4, §4.2) ===
#
# Consumes a gift and produces a verdict. The verdict discharges
# through @kintsugi/consent's three-state floor (Pass | Partial |
# Fail). Accept is the receiver's sovereign act; decline is admissible
# and produces Fail(declinable_note).
#
# Substrate-decl invariant per A4: accept MUST consult @kintsugi/consent
# per @gift.gift_declinable (§1.5). No new @gift-specific consent
# species is minted; the three-state floor is sufficient.
accept(g: gift, receiver: subject_or_substrate) -> verdict { \ }

# === attribute — the load-bearing walker (§1.3, §1.4) ===
#
# Given a gift's OID, returns the giver's subject_instance.
# Constant-time via content-addressing; the giver is the first field
# of the gift record and the gift's OID is byte-visible in every
# downstream composition.
#
# Discharges §1.3 anti-extraction claim: the substrate's audit
# surface (per @kintsugi/store/git) preserves the giver-chain as
# byte-visible git objects; attribute walks the chain.
attribute(g: gift) -> subject_instance { \ }

# === attribute_composition — reverse-closure walker (§1.4) ===
#
# Given a composition's OID that transitively consumed one or more
# gifts, returns the set of givers. Walks @mirror/store.impacted_by
# in reverse; for every gift-OID in the closure, reads the giver
# field. The substrate-decl form of "who do I owe attribution to
# for this composition?"
attribute_composition(composition_oid: ref) -> [subject_instance] { \ }

# === pay_forward — Landing 3 §17.4 positive obligation ===
#
# Given a received gift `received` (the receiver has accepted it), a
# new receiver `new_receiver`, and a new artifact `new_artifact`
# (typically a composition or transform of the received gift's
# artifact), constructs a new gift whose giver is the previous
# receiver and whose ancestry preserves the received gift's chain.
#
# pay_forward is NOT reciprocity: the new gift's receiver is NOT the
# original giver; the new gift does NOT close a loop. It OPENS a
# loop toward the next receiver. The received gift's giver-chain is
# preserved in the new gift's ancestry field per §1.4.
#
# pay_forward is admissible whenever `received.accept` discharged
# Pass. Attempting pay_forward on a declined gift is a substrate-
# decl error (nothing was received; there is nothing to pay forward).
#
# The three sub-conditions the substrate enforces at pay_forward:
#
#   1. received.accept(receiver=new_receiver.giver) was Pass
#      (only accepted gifts are pay-forwardable)
#   2. new_artifact composes over received.artifact
#      (the pay-forward carries the received gift's substance
#      forward; unrelated artifacts are admissible as their own
#      gifts, not as pay-forwards)
#   3. new_receiver != received.giver
#      (paying forward TO the original giver is reciprocation;
#      substrate refuses reciprocation at substrate-decl per §1.5
#      invariant 3; use @gift.offer with ancestry-cite for a
#      response-gift shape at a distinct altitude)
#
# NOTE (Seam D9 adjudication, per docs/specs/subject-bauchladen-
# visibility-and-eigenboard-loop.md Landing 4): pay_forward respects
# @subject/visibility scopes (visibility_scope.can_be_elevated_to
# gates propagation; private artifacts cannot be paid forward without
# subject-authorized elevation via query_phi). Subject-absent branch
# defers per Landing 2 interaction-loop discipline; back-integration
# into pay_forward signature forward-promised as A25 for Landing 5+.
#
# Body discharges per-realisation: constructs a new gift record whose
# giver = received.receiver (upgraded to subject_instance), receiver =
# new_receiver, artifact = new_artifact, ancestry = received.oid
# (chained), timestamp = @time/monotonic at call, attribution_note
# carrying the pay-forward reference.
pay_forward(received:         gift,
            new_receiver:     subject_or_substrate,
            new_artifact:     ref,
            attribution_note: nl,
            declinable_note:  nl) -> gift { \ }

# === pay_forward_chain — enumerate the pay-forward ancestry (§17.4) ===
#
# Given a gift's OID, walks the ancestry field recursively (each
# ancestor is either a pay-forward's `received` or a null-terminator
# at a substrate-inaugural gift per §17.3). Returns the ordered list
# of gifts from the substrate-inaugural gift to the current gift.
#
# Discharges the substrate origin postulate (§17.3): for any current
# gift, the pay-forward chain terminates at a substrate-inaugural
# gift; the chain is walkable in O(chain-length) via content-address
# resolution.
pay_forward_chain(g: gift) -> gift_set { \ }

# === Bilateral predicates — the five structural invariants (§1.5) ===
#
# Each returns verdict per @glass. Discharged bounded at substrate-
# decl altitude; consumed by any composition that reads a gift.

# attribution_preserved: for gift g and downstream composition c,
# does canonical(c) contain canonical(g.giver.identity_oid) as a
# byte-substring? Bounded lookup; discharges immediately.
attribution_preserved(g: gift, c: ref) -> verdict { \ }

# use_rights_transferred: does the receiver's identity_oid appear in
# the substrate's authorized-consumer set for artifact g.artifact?
# Discharged via @kintsugi/store's access-control surface. Standard
# form: Pass unless the giver explicitly gated (see §8 A7 on whether
# @gift admits gated variants; Mara-recommended NO).
use_rights_transferred(g: gift) -> verdict { \ }

# no_reciprocity_expected: does g carry any reciprocity marker?
# @gift substrate-decl invariant: gifts carry no reciprocity marker
# by construction; this predicate discharges Pass by definition,
# and returns Fail if a downstream composition tries to add a
# reciprocity marker (which the substrate refuses per §1.5 anti-
# reciprocity structural claim).
no_reciprocity_expected(g: gift) -> verdict { \ }

# gift_declinable: does g.declinable_note resolve to a non-empty
# refusal path? Discharged bounded; Pass iff the note declares a
# structurally admissible decline path. Fail if the giver has
# elided the note without providing one.
gift_declinable(g: gift) -> verdict { \ }

# composition_honest: for gift g and downstream composition c, do
# c's audit records surface g's attribution? Walked via
# @kintsugi/store/git; discharges bounded. THE substrate-decl form
# of Hyde 1983 "the gift moves": the attribution flows forward under
# every composition, not backward as debt.
composition_honest(g: gift, c: ref) -> verdict { \ }

# === substrate_inaugural — Landing 3 §17.4 well-foundedness ===
#
# For a gift g, discharges Pass iff g has no prior gift in its
# ancestry (g IS a substrate-inaugural gift — the first-gift of the
# substrate, or the first-gift under a substrate-as-giver cycle per
# §12). Fail iff g's ancestry field resolves to a prior gift.
#
# Substrate-decl invariant: for any mirror substrate S at time t,
# there exists at least one gift g such that substrate_inaugural(g)
# discharges Pass; the substrate's origin chain is well-founded.
# Alex Wolf's first-gift (2026-07-14) IS the mirror substrate's
# inaugural gift per §18.4.
substrate_inaugural(g: gift) -> verdict { \ }

# === gift_witnessing — the composed bilateral ===
#
# The load-bearing bilateral consumers cite in `requires` clauses.
# Discharges all five structural invariants + substrate_inaugural
# well-foundedness conjointly. Follows the substrate's `X_witnessing`
# pattern established at @bauchladen, @autopoietic, @fate,
# @cybernetic/eigenform, @cybernetic/second_order, @torus.
gift_witnessing(g: gift) -> verdict { \ }

out @gift
out subject_or_substrate
out gift
out gift_set
out offer
out accept
out attribute
out attribute_composition
out pay_forward
out pay_forward_chain
out attribution_preserved
out use_rights_transferred
out no_reciprocity_expected
out gift_declinable
out composition_honest
out substrate_inaugural
out gift_witnessing
